hi my name is Adam Cohen I'm a professor  in the department's of chemistry and  physics at Harvard today I'm going to  talk to you about a huge unsolved  problem of how to visualize electrical  activity in the brain now take a minute  and put your hand on your head and think  about what you're touching within a  centimeter of your fingers there's a  piece of the most mysterious and poorly  understood matter in the universe here's  a picture of your brain now if we zoom  in by a factor of about 300 we see that  this brain is made up of neurons there's  about a hundred billion of them and  they're connected to each other each by  about ten to the four ten thousand  connections if we zoom in again now by a  factor of ten thousand we see that each  one of these neurons is encased in a  lipid bilayer a little layer of grease  which separates the inside of the cell  from the outside of the cell this  bilayer is an electrical insulator while  the cytoplasm and the extracellular  medium are conductors so you can think  of it a little bit like a parallel plate  capacitor now there are many proteins  that sit in the membrane and these  proteins can pass ions across the  membrane there are charge pumps which  can generate a voltage and there are ion  channels which can relieve this voltage  these channels act a little bit like  resistors except they're nonlinear so if  there's a change in the voltage they can  have big changes in the conductance  which can lead to these electrical  spikes in the membrane voltage which we  call action potentials every action  potential is about a tenth of a volt I  and last about one one thousandth of a  second these are the currency of the  nervous system so every thought and hope  and dream and feeling that we have is  encoded in these patterns of electrical  spikes traveling through the hundred  billion neurons in our brain that's all  there is and so we'd really like to see  these action potentials so that we can  understand what each neuron is doing and  also to look at the global patterns of  activity and the interrelations of the  signals between neurons to understand  how information is being processed  within the nervous system so basically  this is a voltage measurement problem  now traditionally this is measured as  you might measure the voltage in  electrical circuit electrophysiologists  take a glass pipette with a little  electrode in it and jam the pipette into  a cell and then there's another  electrode out in the solution and by  hooking these up to an amplifier they  can measure the voltage difference and  this works nicely but it only measures  one cell at a time it's extremely slow  and laborious to get these electrodes  into the cell and it doesn't give you  the global view of what's going on in a  complex neural circuit here's an  artist's conception of what we'd really  like to do we'd like to just make these  neurons light up when they fire the  problem is you can't see the voltage in  a neuron anymore then you can see the  voltage in a telephone cord when  somebody's having a telephone  conversation we need some contrast agent  to convert membrane voltage into light  now physicists and chemists and  biologists have been working on trying  to invent contrast agents since the late  1960s and this activity has really taken  off in the last few years here are just  a few of the things that people are  working on today these are all from  within the last year or so people are  trying to develop voltage sensitive dyes  so these are organic small molecules  which will intercalate into the membrane  and in some of these dyes illumination  with light generates an electronic  excited state the charge distribution in  the excited state is different than a  charge distribution in the ground state  and so when there's a voltage across the  membrane that voltage perturbs the  relative energy of these two states  which can alter the fluorescent  properties of the dot and so this  provides a means to readout membrane  voltage with fluorescence other people  are trying to put semiconductor nano  crystals in lipid membranes these nano  crystals have quantum mechanical  delocalized electronic states membrane  voltage can again shift the relative  energies of these states and adjust  again the fluorescence properties of  these crystals other people are working  with objects called nitrogen vacancies  in diamonds these are tiny quantum  mechanical defects in diamonds and  people think that if you get a small  enough diamond nano crystal and you  stick it into a membrane the  fluorescence properties of these  nitrogen vacancies will again be  sensitive to  in voltage now I want to emphasize these  are all interesting perspective  technologies but none of these has  solved the problem that I posed at the  beginning yet in all of these  technologies the sensor is sitting in  the membrane and this is because to  measure a voltage difference you need to  be connected both to the inside of the  cell and the outside of the cell other  people including myself are working on  voltage indicators based on fluorescent  transmembrane proteins the idea is to  have a protein which sits in the  membrane and to somehow make its  fluorescence sensitive to the membrane  voltage I'll tell you just a little bit  about one indicator which we've been  developing in my lab the story starts  here this is a picture of the dead sea  there is a micro organism that lives in  the water here that produces a  transmembrane protein called r2 adopts  and 3 this protein absorbs sunlight and  uses that energy to pump a proton a  charge from inside the cell to outside  the cell this generates a membrane  voltage which this creature uses to  power its metabolism so a few years ago  I said to myself I wonder if we can run  this thing in Reverse can we use a  change in membrane voltage to induce a  conformational change in this protein  which will lead to a fluorescent signal  that we can see just like this right and  you see this works in power plunk and so  that's very encouraging after several  years we engineered a protein which had  these attributes and here's a picture of  a human tumor cell where we've  introduced a gene for this protein and  we're using a little patch pipette which  you can't see to induce steps of voltage  between plus and minus 1/10 of a volt  and we're looking at the fluorescence of  the protein so here you can see that as  the voltage goes up the protein gets  bright and as the voltage goes down the  protein gets dim so this is a  genetically encoded fluorescent reporter  a membrane voltage and it turns out to  be quite fast and sensitive but also  very dim which makes it hard to measure  we've now developed technology to take  these proteins and to express them in  neurons in a petri dish so the neurons  become fluorescent and their  fluorescence becomes sensitive to the  voltage in the membrane  we're also expressing another protein in  these cells which renders them sensitive  to blue light this is a light gated ion  channel and targeting a flash of blue  light to the cell body will trigger the  neuron to fire we've developed  computational techniques to infer the  propagation of these electrical impulses  at extremely high time resolution here  I'll show you a movie at a hundred  thousand frames a second of this  optically induced action potential  propagating through a nerve so you can  really see how these signals propagate  through a cell now but this is a neuron  in culture we've taken the rat brain and  we've mooshed it up and we spread out  the neurons in a dish we'd really like  to do this in an intact brain but  neither we nor anybody else on the  planet knows how to do that right now  there are several challenges to this and  I'll tell you about them so one of the  problems we face is that the brain is  not transparent here's a picture of a  mouse brain and you can see it's  scatters light very strongly it's about  the size of my pinky but light scatters  over a distance less than a tenth of a  millimeter and so it's an unsolved  challenge how to visualize what's going  on inside of this chunk of tissue we  need to come up with new kinds of  optical or electrical tricks another  challenge is that in every mouse brain  there's about 75 million neurons to  record from these at a thousand frames a  second which you need to detect action  potentials would lead to a data rate of  about 75 gigabytes a second we don't  know how to store this data let alone  what to do with it if we did have it so  this brings me to another unsolved  challenge which is the interpretation  problem so even if we had the best  technology imaginable for visualizing  electrical activity in the brain how do  we interpret it we'd like to relate the  electrical activity to some statement  about the underlying biology of the  cells what are the ion channels that are  active what's their state of regulation  what's their distribution what's the  metabolic state of the cell what's  happening to its energy supply we all  experience that  we go through our day night cycle the  function of our neurons changes  tremendously whether we're asleep or  awake tired or not tired hungry or not  hungry and those changes are not  mediated by structural changes in the  brain but by changes in presumably the  metabolic state of our neurons and then  finally we'd like to infer from patterns  of activity how the neurons are  connected to each other to figure out  who is calling the shots and who is  controlling them and how they integrate  and process information to illustrate  this here is a movie of some neuron this  was a movie taken in my lab these are  neurons in a dish not in an intact brain  where we'll look at their firing  patterns this is a movie at about 500  frames a second the neurons have been  artificially colored just so you can  tell them apart and we can zoom in on  any one of these cells and look at its  firing pattern and we really don't know  how to interpret these firing patterns  in terms of the ion channels the  energetics and the connectivity of these  cells so there's tremendous scope over  the coming years to combine the efforts  of physicists chemists and biologists to  develop technology which will let us  visualize the activity in this little  chunk of matter which is by between all  of our ears
 

